    {
      "dom_id": "question-127026",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/assets/28a7eb32cfcd800c907e9f4af927eca7bd8616d36162682413db62a9feabefbb.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive",
      "worked_solution": "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/assets/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/assets/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/assets/fecf59674259d41222ffd981b1ab6d90c474fe2b0e37a6d5f7626f34536a6896.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/assets/56a7fc09ed2a2a15b0949fa6041dc9c0ee6beb69f30b1a5c048afd8c57887d52.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
