  "canonical_examples": [
    {
      "dom_id": "step-184958",
      "name": "Example: \n                    Determining a Flux's Sign From a Diagram",
      "problem": "Consider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown below. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is positive\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/assets/7e7fbe6aaf1310b186344a3cf5a127075f11a86b51db2e687ddc7035d5d890f8.png)",
      "worked_solution": "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $\\mathbf F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C\\mathbin:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/assets/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero, the flux equals zero.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/assets/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nThe outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below we see that $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to $\\mathbf n$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/assets/4a3d9864bb6bada1fd218b299427bc5bb4ffcbcccb1a504fff77416d5d292151.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/assets/9310d17389f61de2984c0e23cfe1060e77d4a87a140d2f1c892bdbab9b99629b.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [],
      "problem_field_keys": [],
      "answer_fields_complete": null,
