      ],
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/observations/lesson-step-184956-437c279c5615f14fd29d.html",
      "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/observations/lesson-step-184956-437c279c5615f14fd29d.png",
      "source_url": "https://mathacademy.com/topics/3715",
      "captured_at": 1791746126.9304543,
      "math_academy_id": 9522,
      "title": "Constructing an Integral Formula for the Flux",
      "content": "If $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ The symbol $\\oint\\limits$ means integration over the closed curve.\n\nIt's helpful to spend some time understanding where this definition comes from, so let's derive it using the closed curve $C$ below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/assets/6dfe8a071798c93f19507808596b7d32af90367b2666643d092a7c50afe5e929.png)\n\nFirst, we pick a point $P$ on $C.$ At this point, we draw the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the outward-pointing normal vector $\\mathbf n$ to $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/assets/498e1afbaac8bc8297213eae09cc8bf8742b29f45dda1dfafff868c67ebb9de9.png)\n\nWe wish to calculate the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C$ at $P.$ Now, $\\mathbf F$ is made up of two components:\n\n- a component parallel to $\\mathbf n,$ which contributes to the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C,$ and\n\n- a component perpendicular to $\\mathbf n,$ which does *not* contribute to the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C.$\n\nWe're only interested in the contribution made to the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C.$ This is given by the scalar projection of $\\mathbf F$ in the direction of $\\mathbf n.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/assets/ac3aae49f372908f2f9aeb53e3fc98bff117846fca05a8c696009e443390811c.png)\n\nTherefore, the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C$ at $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{n}F & =\\frac{F\\cdot n}{||n||}=F\\cdot n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression above represents the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C$ at a single point only. Therefore, to calculate the total net flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C,$ we sum (integrate) this expression over the entire curve. This gives\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s.\n$$"
    },
    {
      "dom_id": "step-184957",
      "name": "Orientation-Independence",
      "problem": "Consider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F$ below. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/assets/e679efaa2612d45bbfe8b918de7148853b64eaf530af34a525ef1735e0946b09.png)\n\nBy drawing an outward-pointing unit normal vector, it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/assets/fbcd6fd0fadcc955212a145de9edb4654ddb92ddebb8ba31eb775cff676efeeb.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is positive, and we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s > 0.\n$$\n\nWhen measuring flux through a closed curve, the curve's orientation does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same in either case. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/assets/a3d398d382e6bc94ddde77e650d50a6b5ec050083f89f64104f6f2fd172a9239.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is also positive, and we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s > 0.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
