      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/observations/lesson-step-184957-9829b534271f8e34f238.html",
      "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/observations/lesson-step-184957-9829b534271f8e34f238.png",
      "source_url": "https://mathacademy.com/topics/3715",
      "captured_at": 1791746128.348906,
      "math_academy_id": 9523,
      "title": "Orientation-Independence",
      "content": "Consider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F$ below. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/assets/e679efaa2612d45bbfe8b918de7148853b64eaf530af34a525ef1735e0946b09.png)\n\nBy drawing an outward-pointing unit normal vector, it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/assets/fbcd6fd0fadcc955212a145de9edb4654ddb92ddebb8ba31eb775cff676efeeb.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is positive, and we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s > 0.\n$$\n\nWhen measuring flux through a closed curve, the curve's orientation does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same in either case. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/assets/a3d398d382e6bc94ddde77e650d50a6b5ec050083f89f64104f6f2fd172a9239.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is also positive, and we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s > 0.\n$$"
    },
    {
      "dom_id": "step-184961",
      "name": "Calculating Flux",
      "problem": "To calculate the flux of a vector field over a smooth, closed curve, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nIf $C=C_1\\cup C_2 \\cup \\cdots \\cup C_n$ is piecewise smooth, we have to calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ over each smooth face. That is,\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s  =  \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+ \\cdots +\n\\int\\limits_{C_n} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ on $C_i.$\n\nAs an example, let's consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = xy\\,\\mathbf i + (y-x)^3\\,\\mathbf j$ and the rectangle $C$ shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/assets/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\n \n\nTo calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ over $C,$ we sum the fluxes of $\\mathbf F$ over each side:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate how to compute these integrals, let's construct the flux integral across the side $C_4,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nThe side $C_4$ and its outward-pointing unit normal are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123192/assets/06fd5a2ecbd709e8e835f2a57ef3ada772526366d51e5e16474d9b8635500e8e.png)\n\nSince this side is perpendicular to the $y$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have \n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = -\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}(xy\\,i+(y-x)^{3}\\,j)\\cdot (-j)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}(xy\\,i)\\cdot (-j)+((y-x)^{3}\\,j))\\cdot (-j)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}0-(y-x)^{3}\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}(x-y)^{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFollowing a similar procedure for the other sides, we can construct the full expression for the flux of $\\mathbf F$ over $C.$",
      "worked_solution": "",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
